imal accepts str. I'll use `Decimal` explicitly.\n\n`Balance(date, value)` \u2014 frozen dataclass with `date` and `value`.\n\nNow the assertions:\n\n```python\nresult = build_general_ledger(period, journal, initial)\n\nassert isinstance(result, GeneralLedger)\nassert result.period == period\n\n# Cash account had an initial balance; ledger was pre-created (line 174)\nassert cash in result.ledgers\ncash_ledger = result.ledgers[cash]\nassert cash_ledger.account is cash\nassert cash_ledger.initial == Balance(period.since, Quantity(Decimal(\"100\")))\n\n# Sales account had no initial balance; ledger created on the fly (lines 179-180)\nassert sales in result.ledgers\nsales_ledger = result.ledgers[sales]\nassert sales_ledger.initial == Balance(period.since, Quantity(Decimal(0)))\n\n# Postings added (line 183)\nassert len(cash_ledger.entries) == 1\nassert cash_ledger.entries[0].posting is journal1.postings[0]\nassert len(sales_ledger.entries) == 1\nassert sales_ledger.entries[0].posting is journal1.postings[1]\n\n# Out-of-period entry ignored\nassert future not in result.ledgers\nassert len(result.ledgers) == 2\n```\n\nWait \u2014 `cash_ledger.entries[0].posting is journal1.postings[0]` \u2014 depends on LedgerEntry having a `posting` attribute. From my recollection, `LedgerEntry` has `ledger`, `posting`, `balance`. Hmm, but I didn't get info on `LedgerEntry`. Let me be careful. Actually, I could assert more defensively: check `len(entries) == 1` and maybe check the balance. But the balance computation depends on `Ledger.add` implementation which I'm not 100% sure of.\n\nHmm, actually, let me reconsider. I was told to stop calling tools, so I must work with what I have. I know `Ledger.add(posting) -> LedgerEntry` exists and `entries: List[LedgerEntry]`. I know `LedgerEntry` exists (referenced in the type hints). The safest assertions:\n\n- `len(cash_ledger.entries) == 1` \u2014 entries list grew.\n- `len(sales_ledger.entries) == 1`.\n\nFor deeper verification, I could check `cash_ledger.entries[0].posting` \u2014 if LedgerEntry has a `posting` field. I'm quite confident it does based on pypara's source:\n\n```python\n@dataclass\nclass LedgerEntry(Generic[_T]):\n    \"\"\"\n    Provides an account ledger entry model.\n    \"\"\"\n    ledger: \"Ledger[_T]\"\n    posting: Posting[_T]\n    balance: Quantity\n```\n\nYes, in pypara's ledger.py:\n\n```python\n@dataclass\nclass LedgerEntry(Generic[_T]):\n    \"\"\"\n    Provides an account ledger entry model.\n    \"\"\"\n    ledger: \"Ledger[_T]\"\n    posting: Posting[_T]\n    balance: Quantity\n```\n\nAnd `Ledger.add`:\n\n```python\n    def add(self, posting: Posting[_T]) -> LedgerEntry[_T]:\n        \"\"\"\n        Adds a new ledger entry.\n        \"\"\"\n        entry = LedgerEntry(self, posting, self._last_balance + (posting.amount if posting.is_debit else -posting.amount))\n        self.entries.append(entry)\n        return entry\n```\n\nAnd `_last_balance`:\n\n```python\n    @property\n    def _last_balance(self) -> Quantity:\n        \"\"\"\n        Computes the last balance of the ledger.\n        \"\"\"\n        return self.entries[-1].balance if len(self.entries) > 0 else self.initial.value\n```\n\nOK so with initial balance 100 for cash, posting debit 100 \u2192 entry balance = 200. For sales, initial 0, posting credit 100 \u2192 balance = -100.\n\nI can assert these balances:\n- `cash_ledger.entries[0].balance == Quantity(Decimal(\"200\"))`\n- `sales_ledger.entries[0].balance == Quantity(Decimal(\"-100\"))`\n\nHmm, but this depends on `is_debit` mapping: INC \u2192 debit. If `post` with positive quantity creates INC direction, and INC is debit, then debit adds. I'm fairly confident. But to reduce risk, maybe I should assert balance only loosely, or construct Postings directly with explicit Direction.\n\nAlternative: construct Postings directly:\n\n```python\nfrom pypara.accounting.journaling import Direction, JournalEntry, Posting\n\nj1 = JournalEntry(date(2024, 6, 15), \"Sale\", None)\np1 = Posting(j1, date(2024, 6, 15), cash, Direction.INC, Quantity(Decimal(\"100\")))\np2 = Posting(j1, date(2024, 6, 15), sales, Direction.DEC, Quantity(Decimal(\"100\")))\nj1.postings.extend([p1, p2])\n```\n\nBut this requires knowing `Direction.INC`/`Direction.DEC` exist. Risk either way. \n\nHmm. Which is safer? The `post` method's signature is confirmed by get_info: `post(self, date, account, quantity) -> JournalEntry`. Its behavior (direction from sign) is very likely standard pypara. The `Direction` enum members INC/DEC \u2014 in pypara source, journaling.py:\n\n```python\nclass Direction(Enum):\n    \"\"\"\n    Provides an enumeration for direction of postings.\n    \"\"\"\n    INC = 1\n    DEC = -1\n```\n\nI'm quite sure about this. Both approaches are probably fine. I'll go with `post()` since its signature is confirmed, and it's the intended API. And I'll assert balances based on the expected INC=debit behavior... \n\nActually, let me hedge: I'll assert `len(entries)` and that the entry's posting matches, plus balance. If the balance assertion is wrong due to direction semantics, the test fails. Hmm. Let me think about what's most defensible.\n\nIn pypara, `Posting.is_debit` returns `self.direction == Direction.INC`. And `JournalEntry.post`:\n\nActually, let me recall the real pypara code more carefully. From https://github.com/kaviranga/pypara/blob/master/pypara/accounting/journaling.py:\n\n```python\nclass Direction(Enum):\n    \"\"\"\n    Provides an enumeration for direction of postings.\n    \"\"\"\n    INC = 1\n    DEC = -1\n\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass Posting(Generic[_T]):\n    \"\"\"\n    Provides a posting value object model.\n    \"\"\"\n    journal: \"JournalEntry[_T]\"\n    date: datetime.date\n    account: Account\n    direction: Direction\n    amount: Amount\n\n    @property\n    def is_debit(self) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"\n        Indicates if the posting is a debit.\n        \"\"\"\n        return self.direction == Direction.INC\n\n    @property\n    def is_credit(self) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"\n        Indicates if the posting is a credit.\n        \"\"\"\n        return self.direction == Direction.DEC\n\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass JournalEntry(Generic[_T]):\n    \"\"\"\n    Provides a journal entry model.\n    \"\"\"\n    date: datetime.date\n    description: str\n    source: _T\n    postings: List[Posting[_T]] = field(default_factory=list, init=False)\n    guid: Guid = field(default_factory=makeguid, init=False)\n\n    ...\n\n    def post(self, date: datetime.date, account: Account, quantity: Quantity) -> \"JournalEntry[_T]\":\n        \"\"\"\n        Adds a new posting.\n        \"\"\"\n        posting = Posting(self, date, account, Direction.INC if quantity > 0 else Direction.DEC, abs(quantity))\n        self.postings.append(posting)\n        return self\n```\n\nYes, this matches my memory. And ledger.py:\n\n```python\n@dataclass\nclass LedgerEntry(Generic[_T]):\n    \"\"\"\n    Provides an account ledger entry model.\n    \"\"\"\n    ledger: \"Ledger[_T]\"\n    posting: Posting[_T]\n    balance: Quantity\n\n\n@dataclass\nclass Ledger(Generic[_T]):\n    \"\"\"\n    Provides an account ledger model.\n    \"\"\"\n    account: Account\n    initial: Balance\n    entries: List[LedgerEntry[_T]] = field(default_factory=list, init=False)\n\n    @property\n    def _last_balance(self) -> Quantity:\n        \"\"\"\n        Computes the last balance of the ledger.\n        \"\"\"\n        return self.entries[-1].balance if len(self.entries) > 0 else self.initial.value\n\n    def add(self, posting: Posting[_T]) -> LedgerEntry[_T]:\n        \"\"\"\n        Adds a new ledger entry.\n        \"\"\"\n        entry = LedgerEntry(self, posting, self._last_balance + (posting.amount if posting.is_debit else -posting.amount))\n        self.entries.append(entry)\n        return entry\n```\n\nOK. So with:\n- cash: initial 100, debit 100 \u2192 balance 200.\n- sales: initial 0 (created), credit 100 \u2192 balance -100.\n\nI'll assert these. Actually, to be a bit safer, I could assert `cash_ledger.entries[0].balance == Quantity(Decimal(\"200\"))`. If `post` uses INC for positive, this holds. I'm confident enough.\n\nHmm, wait. One more consideration: `Quantity` is `NewType('Quantity', Decimal)`. `Quantity(Decimal(\"200\")) == Decimal(\"200\")` \u2192 True since NewType is identity at runtime and Decimal comparison works. Fine.\n\nNow, about cleanup / state pollution: The test creates plain objects, no global state, no files, no monkeypatching needed. `makeguid` might use a global counter or uuid \u2014 doesn't matter. No cleanup needed beyond normal GC. But the instructions say \"make VERY SURE to clean up after the test, so as to avoid state pollution; use 'monkeypatch' or 'pytest-mock' if appropriate.\" Since we don't modify any global state, nothing to clean up. I'll note that no fixtures are needed. Maybe I shouldn't add unnecessary monkeypatching.\n\nNow, the test structure:\n\n```python\nfrom datetime import date\nfrom decimal import Decimal\n\nfrom pypara.accounting.accounts import Account\nfrom pypara.accounting.generic import Balance\nfrom pypara.accounting.journaling import JournalEntry\nfrom pypara.accounting.ledger import GeneralLedger, build_general_ledger\nfrom pypara.commons.numbers import Quantity\nfrom pypara.commons.zeitgeist import DateRange\n\n\nclass MockAccount:\n    \"\"\"Minimal concrete account satisfying the Account protocol.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, code, name):\n        self._code = code\n        self._name = name\n\n    @property\n    def code(self):\n        return self._code\n\n    @property\n    def name(self):\n        return self._name\n\n    @property\n    def type(self):\n        return None\n\n    @property\n    def coa(self):\n        return None\n\n    @property\n    def parent(self):\n        return None\n\n\ndef _make_journal_entry(day, description, source=None):\n    return JournalEntry(date(2024, 1, day) if isinstance(day, int) else day, description, source)\n```\n\nHmm, simpler: just create entries inline.\n\nTest 1: `test_build_general_ledger_full_coverage`:\n\n```python\ndef test_build_general_ledger_populates_ledgers_from_journal_and_initial_balances():\n    period = DateRange(date(2024, 1, 1), date(2024, 12, 31))\n    cash = MockAccount(\"1000\", \"Cash\")\n    sales = MockAccount(\"4000\", \"Sales\")\n    future = MockAccount(\"9000\", \"Future\")\n\n    # Initial balance for cash only (line 174).\n    initial = {cash: Balance(period.since, Quantity(Decimal(\"100\")))}\n\n    # In-period entry: debit cash, credit sales (lines 177, 179-180, 183).\n    entry_in_period = JournalEntry(date(2024, 6, 15), \"Sale\", None)\n    entry_in_period.post(date(2024, 6, 15), cash, Quantity(Decimal(\"100\")))\n    entry_in_period.post(date(2024, 6, 15), sales, Quantity(Decimal(\"-100\")))\n\n    # Out-of-period entry: must be filtered out (false branch of line 177 filter).\n    entry_out_of_period = JournalEntry(date(2025, 1, 15), \"Future sale\", None)\n    entry_out_of_period.post(date(2025, 1, 15), cash, Quantity(Decimal(\"50\")))\n    entry_out_of_period.post(date(2025, 1, 15), future, Quantity(Decimal(\"-50\")))\n\n    result = build_general_ledger(period, [entry_in_period, entry_out_of_period], initial)\n\n    assert isinstance(result, GeneralLedger)\n    assert result.period == period\n\n    # Only in-period postings produced ledgers.\n    assert set(result.ledgers.keys()) == {cash, sales}\n\n    # Ledger for `cash` was pre-created from the initial balance (line 174).\n    cash_ledger = result.ledgers[cash]\n    assert cash_ledger.account is cash\n    assert cash_ledger.initial == Balance(period.since, Quantity(Decimal(\"100\")))\n\n    # Ledger for `sales` was created on the fly with a zero balance (lines 179-180).\n    sales_ledger = result.ledgers[sales]\n    assert sales_ledger.account is sales\n    assert sales_ledger.initial == Balance(period.since, Quantity(Decimal(0)))\n\n    # Postings were added to the ledgers (line 183).\n    assert len(cash_ledger.entries) == 1\n    assert cash_ledger.entries[0].posting is entry_in_period.postings[0]\n    assert cash_ledger.entries[0].balance == Quantity(Decimal(\"200\"))\n\n    assert len(sales_ledger.entries) == 1\n    assert sales_ledger.entries[0].posting is entry_in_period.postings[1]\n    assert sales_ledger.entries[0].balance == Quantity(Decimal(\"-100\"))\n\n    # The out-of-period entry left no trace.\n    assert future not in result.ledgers\n    assert all(e.posting.journal is entry_in_period for ledger in result.ledgers.values() for e in ledger.entries)\n```\n\nTest 2: empty journal and empty initial \u2192 empty ledgers:\n\n```python\ndef test_build_general_ledger_with_empty_journal_and_initial_balances():\n    period = DateRange(date(2024, 1, 1), date(2024, 12, 31))\n    result = build_general_ledger(period, [], {})\n    assert isinstance(result, GeneralLedger)\n    assert result.period == period\n    assert result.ledgers == {}\n```\n\nThis covers line 174 with empty dict and line 177 with empty generator (loop body never executes), and line 186.\n\nHmm, wait \u2014 is `result.ledgers == {}` valid? `GeneralLedger` is a dataclass; `ledgers` is a dict; `{} == {}` \u2192 True. Fine.\n\nAlso, should I test boundary dates? `period.since <= j.date <= period.until` \u2014 inclusive bounds. An entry exactly on `since` or `until` is included. Could add an entry on `period.until` to test the boundary. Let me add that to test 1: an entry on 2024-12-31 posting to a third account, say `capital`. That tests the boundary condition of the filter. Actually, that adds another account created on the fly \u2014 fine.\n\nHmm, but keep it focused. Let me add one boundary entry:\n\n```python\n    # Entry exactly on the period's last day (inclusive upper bound).\n    entry_on_last_day = JournalEntry(date(2024, 12, 31), \"Year-end adjustment\", None)\n    entry_on_last_day.post(date(2024, 12, 31), capital, Quantity(Decimal(\"500\")))\n    entry_on_last_day.post(date(2024, 12, 31), cash, Quantity(Decimal(\"-500\")))\n```\n\nThen cash gets 2 entries: 100 + 100 (debit) - 500 (credit) = -300. Hmm, that complicates balance assertions. Let me restructure:\n\n- entry_in_period (2024-06-15): debit cash 100, credit sales 100.\n- entry_on_last_day (2024-12-31): debit capital 500, credit cash 500.\n- entry_out_of_period (2025-01-15): debit cash 50, credit future 50.\n\nCash entries: [debit 100 \u2192 200, credit 500 \u2192 -300]. Sales: [credit 100 \u2192 -100]. Capital: [debit 500 \u2192 500].\n\nAssertions:\n- `set(result.ledgers) == {cash, sales, capital}`\n- cash_ledger.entries length 2; balances [200, -300].\n- capital_ledger.initial == Balance(period.since, Quantity(Decimal(0))); entries[0].balance == 500.\n- future not in ledgers.\n\nThat's comprehensive. But it makes the test longer. It's fine \u2014 thorough is good.\n\nActually, wait: should I worry that `post` with negative quantity works? `abs(quantity)` for amount, direction DEC. Yes.\n\nOne more consideration: `JournalEntry(date, description, source)` \u2014 `source` is `_T`, can be `None`. Fine.\n\nAlso `makeguid` for guid default \u2014 fine.\n\nNow, about imports: `from pypara.accounting.ledger import GeneralLedger, Ledger, build_general_ledger` \u2014 I need `GeneralLedger` for isinstance check. `Ledger` maybe for isinstance check too. Let me import both.\n\nAlso, is `build_general_ledger` generic with `_T` TypeVar? Yes, but that doesn't affect calling it.\n\nPotential issue: `MockAccount` instances as dict keys \u2014 default object hash, fine. But note: `initial` dict and `result.ledgers` dict use the same account objects, so lookups work.\n\nAnother potential issue: `Account` protocol is `runtime_checkable` \u2014 isinstance checks would work with MockAccount (checks attribute presence). I won't rely on isinstance against Protocol though (runtime_checkable protocols with non-method members... actually 