avior in this version? The get_info confirmed signature `post(self, date: datetime.date, account: Account, quantity: Quantity) -> 'JournalEntry[_T]'`. Behavior: standard pypara. I'll trust it.\n\nHmm, actually, one more risk: what if `post` requires the quantity to be non-zero or validates? `validate()` is separate. OK.\n\nAlternative safer approach: construct `Posting` directly and append to `j.postings`. This requires `Direction`. Risk: Direction member names. I recall pypara's Direction has INC and DEC. Both approaches have small risks. `post()` is confirmed to exist with confirmed signature; its internal behavior is the only unknown, and it's near-certain (INC if positive else DEC, abs amount). I'll go with `post()`.\n\nActually, hmm, wait. Let me reconsider the balance assertion `cash_ledger.entries[0].balance == Quantity(Decimal(\"200\"))`. This depends on:\n1. `post` creating INC for positive quantity (debit).\n2. `Ledger.add` adding amount for debits.\n3. `_last_balance` returning initial.value when no entries.\n\nAll standard pypara. I'm confident. But if I want to be extra safe, I could assert only `len(entries)` and posting identity, plus maybe balance. The balance assertion strengthens the test (verifies postconditions of `add`). I'll keep it but... if direction semantics were flipped (INC=credit), the test would fail. In pypara, INC is definitely debit (is_debit == direction == INC). Keep it.\n\nActually, let me reduce risk slightly: assert balance for cash entries and sales entry. If wrong, the test fails and I'd need to fix. But I can't iterate (one shot). Hmm. The core requirement is covering lines 174-186 with correct assertions. The balance assertions are postcondition checks. Let me think once more about pypara's actual `Ledger.add`:\n\nFrom pypara source (I've read this project before):\n\n```python\n    def add(self, posting: Posting[_T]) -> LedgerEntry[_T]:\n        \"\"\"\n        Adds a new ledger entry.\n        \"\"\"\n        entry = LedgerEntry(self, posting, self._last_balance + (posting.amount if posting.is_debit else -posting.amount))\n        self.entries.append(entry)\n        return entry\n```\n\nYes. And `_last_balance`:\n\n```python\n    @property\n    def _last_balance(self) -> Quantity:\n        \"\"\"\n        Computes the last balance of the ledger.\n        \"\"\"\n        return self.entries[-1].balance if len(self.entries) > 0 else self.initial.value\n```\n\nYes. OK, confident.\n\nLet me now also double-check `LedgerEntry` field names: `ledger`, `posting`, `balance`. In pypara:\n\n```python\n@dataclass\nclass LedgerEntry(Generic[_T]):\n    \"\"\"\n    Provides an account ledger entry model.\n    \"\"\"\n    ledger: \"Ledger[_T]\"\n    posting: Posting[_T]\n    balance: Quantity\n```\n\nYes.\n\nNow write the final test file:\n\n```python\nfrom datetime import date\nfrom decimal import Decimal\n\nfrom pypara.accounting.generic import Balance\nfrom pypara.accounting.journaling import JournalEntry\nfrom pypara.accounting.ledger import GeneralLedger, Ledger, build_general_ledger\nfrom pypara.commons.numbers import Quantity\nfrom pypara.commons.zeitgeist import DateRange\n\n\nclass MockAccount:\n    \"\"\"\n    Minimal concrete account satisfying the ``Account`` protocol; only used as\n    a dictionary key by the code under test, so identity semantics suffice.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, code: str, name: str) -> None:\n        self._code = code\n        self._name = name\n\n    @property\n    def code(self) -> str:\n        return self._code\n\n    @property\n    def name(self) -> str:\n        return self._name\n\n    @property\n    def type(self):\n        return None\n\n    @property\n    def coa(self):\n        return None\n\n    @property\n    def parent(self):\n        return None\n\n\ndef test_build_general_ledger_covers_initial_balances_in_and_out_of_period_postings():\n    \"\"\"\n    Lines 174-186: ledgers are seeded from initial balances, in-period postings\n    are accumulated (creating ledgers on the fly when needed), and out-of-period\n    journal entries are ignored.\n    \"\"\"\n    period = DateRange(date(2024, 1, 1), date(2024, 12, 31))\n    cash = MockAccount(\"1000\", \"Cash\")\n    sales = MockAccount(\"4000\", \"Sales\")\n    capital = MockAccount(\"3000\", \"Capital\")\n    future = MockAccount(\"9000\", \"Future\")\n\n    ## Line 174: seed ledgers from initial balances (only `cash` has one).\n    initial = {cash: Balance(period.since, Quantity(Decimal(\"100\")))}\n\n    ## Line 177 (true branch) + lines 179-180 (`sales`/`capital` not seeded) + line 183:\n    entry_in_period = JournalEntry(date(2024, 6, 15), \"Sale\", None)\n    entry_in_period.post(date(2024, 6, 15), cash, Quantity(Decimal(\"100\")))\n    entry_in_period.post(date(2024, 6, 15), sales, Quantity(Decimal(\"-100\")))\n\n    ## Boundary: an entry dated exactly on `period.until` is still included.\n    entry_on_last_day = JournalEntry(date(2024, 12, 31), \"Year-end adjustment\", None)\n    entry_on_last_day.post(date(2024, 12, 31), capital, Quantity(Decimal(\"500\")))\n    entry_on_last_day.post(date(2024, 12, 31), cash, Quantity(Decimal(\"-500\")))\n\n    ## Line 177 (false branch): an entry outside the period must be filtered out.\n    entry_out_of_period = JournalEntry(date(2025, 1, 15), \"Future sale\", None)\n    entry_out_of_period.post(date(2025, 1, 15), cash, Quantity(Decimal(\"50\")))\n    entry_out_of_period.post(date(2025, 1, 15), future, Quantity(Decimal(\"-50\")))\n\n    ## Line 186: the general ledger is returned.\n    result = build_general_ledger(period, [entry_in_period, entry_on_last_day, entry_out_of_period], initial)\n\n    ## Postconditions:\n    assert isinstance(result, GeneralLedger)\n    assert result.period == period\n\n    ## Only accounts touched by in-period postings (plus seeded ones) appear;\n    ## the out-of-period entry left no trace.\n    assert set(result.ledgers.keys()) == {cash, sales, capital}\n    assert future not in result.ledgers\n\n    ## `cash` ledger was pre-created from the initial balance (line 174)...\n    cash_ledger = result.ledgers[cash]\n    assert isinstance(cash_ledger, Ledger)\n    assert cash_ledger.account is cash\n    assert cash_ledger.initial == Balance(period.since, Quantity(Decimal(\"100\")))\n\n    ## ...and received both in-period postings (line 183), debits adding and\n    ## credits subtracting from the running balance.\n    assert len(cash_ledger.entries) == 2\n    assert cash_ledger.entries[0].posting is entry_in_period.postings[0]\n    assert cash_ledger.entries[0].balance == Quantity(Decimal(\"200\"))\n    assert cash_ledger.entries[1].posting is entry_on_last_day.postings[1]\n    assert cash_ledger.entries[1].balance == Quantity(Decimal(\"-300\"))\n\n    ## `sales` ledger was created on the fly with a zero opening balance (lines 179-180).\n    sales_ledger = result.ledgers[sales]\n    assert isinstance(sales_ledger, Ledger)\n    assert sales_ledger.account is sales\n    assert sales_ledger.initial == Balance(period.since, Quantity(Decimal(0)))\n    assert len(sales_ledger.entries) == 1\n    assert sales_ledger.entries[0].posting is entry_in_period.postings[1]\n    assert sales_ledger.entries[0].balance == Quantity(Decimal(\"-100\"))\n\n    ## `capital` ledger was also created on the fly (lines 179-180).\n    capital_ledger = result.ledgers[capital]\n    assert capital_ledger.account is capital\n    assert capital_ledger.initial == Balance(period.since, Quantity(Decimal(0)))\n    assert len(capital_ledger.entries) == 1\n    assert capital_ledger.entries[0].posting is entry_on_last_day.postings[0]\n    assert capital_ledger.entries[0].balance == Quantity(Decimal(\"500\"))\n\n    ## No ledger entry originates from the out-of-period journal entry.\n    assert all(\n        entry.posting.journal is not entry_out_of_period\n        for ledger in result.ledgers.values()\n        for entry in ledger.entries\n    )\n\n\ndef test_build_general_ledger_with_e