nt=Mock(name=\"account\"),\n           direction=direction,\n           amount=Mock(name=\"amount\"),\n       )\n\n\n   def test_ledger_entry_is_debit_delegates_to_posting():\n       entry = LedgerEntry(ledger=Mock(name=\"ledger\"), posting=_make_posting(Direction.INC), balance=Mock(name=\"balance\"))\n       assert entry.is_debit is True\n       assert entry.is_debit == entry.posting.is_debit\n\n\n   def test_ledger_entry_is_debit_false_for_credit_posting():\n       entry = LedgerEntry(ledger=Mock(name=\"ledger\"), posting=_make_posting(Direction.DEC), balance=Mock(name=\"balance\"))\n       assert entry.is_debit is False\n       assert entry.is_debit == entry.posting.is_debit\n   ```\n\n   Wait \u2014 `JournalEntry(date, description, source)` \u2014 `source: _T` can be None. Fine. `postings` and `guid` are `init=False` with default factories. Fine.\n\n   `Posting` is frozen dataclass \u2014 keyword construction fine.\n\n   One concern: `Posting(journal=..., ...)` \u2014 the field is named `journal`. Yes.\n\n   Another concern: does `Posting.__post_init__` do anything? In pypara, I don't think so. It's a plain frozen dataclass.\n\n   Hmm, one more consideration: `entry.is_debit is True` \u2014 `Posting.is_debit` returns `self.direction == Direction.INC` which is a real bool, so `is True` works. But to be safe, use `==` or `assert entry.is_debit`. Actually `Direction.INC == Direction.INC` \u2192 True (bool). So `is True` is fine. But if the implementation returned something truthy-but-not-bool... it's annotated `-> bool` and returns comparison result, so it's a bool. I'll use `assert entry.is_debit is True` \u2014 hmm, safer to use `assert entry.is_debit` and `assert not entry.is_debit`. Let me use both value assertions via `==`:\n\n   `assert entry.is_debit == entry.posting.is_debit` \u2014 this verifies delegation. And `assert entry.is_debit is True` / `is False` verifies the actual values. I'll keep `is True`/`is False` since the comparison yields bool.\n\n   Act