ock())` works \u2014 dataclass doesn't validate. Then `Ledger` instance is real. But `LedgerEntry.is_debit` doesn't use `self.ledger` anyway. Using a real `Ledger` with mock fields adds little value but also little risk. I could do:\n\n   ```python\n   from pypara.accounting.ledger import Ledger, LedgerEntry\n   ledger = Ledger(account=Mock(), initial=Mock())\n   entry = LedgerEntry(ledger=ledger, posting=posting, balance=Mock())\n   ```\n\n   That's slightly more realistic. But `Ledger` is `Generic[_T]` too \u2014 fine to instantiate.\n\n   Hmm, but there's a subtle risk: does `Ledger` have `__post_init__`? From get_info, it shows fields and `_last_balance` property and `add` method. No `__post_init__` shown. The \"...\" indicates omitted code though \u2014 the get_info output showed the full class I think (fields, `_last_balance`, `add`). It said `\"...\" below indicates omitted code` at the top, meaning there might be more. Risky? The dataclass decorator with fields `account`, `initial`, `entries` \u2014 instantiation `Ledger(account, initial)` should work. I'll use it for realism but it's not essential. Actually, to minimize risk of test failure, maybe just use Mock for ledger. Hmm.\n\n   Let me think about what maximizes correctness certainty while improving coverage:\n\n   Must-have: `LedgerEntry` instance + access `.is_debit`. \n   - `LedgerEntry(ledger, posting, balance)` \u2014 all three fields known from get_info. \n   - `posting` real `Posting` \u2014 constructor known from get_info. Needs `Direction` value \u2014 `Direction.INC`/`DEC` from pypara source knowledge. \n   - `journal` real `JournalEntry` \u2014 constructor known.\n   - `account`, `amount`, `balance`, `ledger` \u2014 Mocks (constructors unknown/irrelevant).\n\n   This is solid. Let me also double check `JournalEntry` construction: `JournalEntry(date, description, source)` \u2014 `postings` and `guid` are `field(init=False)` with default_factory, so they're set automatically. Good.\n\n   One more consideration: `Posting` frozen dataclass \u2014 equality/hash? Frozen dataclass generates `__hash__`. Mocks are hashable. Fine.\n\n   Also, should the test import `Quantity` for balance? `Quantity` constructor unknown \u2014 Mock is safer.\n\n   Alright, also consider: maybe I should test both `is_debit` True and False cases in one test or two tests. Two tests is cleaner. Or one test with both. I'll write two test functions (or one function covering both). The task says \"Create new pytest test functions\" (plural). I'll write two.\n\n   Also, maybe add a test that verifies `is_debit` matches `posting.is_debit` for both directions \u2014 parametrize? Keep it simple with two functions.\n\n   Let me also think: is there any chance `LedgerEntry` has `__post_init__` that touches things? get_info showed the class with fields and properties; the \"...\" at top suggests omitted code above (imports). The properties shown are `date`, `description`, `amount`, `cntraccts`, `is_debit`, `is_credit`, `debit`, `credit`. No `__post_init__` shown. The dataclass has no defaults beyond the three fields, so `LedgerEntry(ledger, posting, balance)` works.\n\n   Hmm wait, actually in real pypara, `LedgerEntry` is:\n\n   ```python\n   @dataclass\n   class LedgerEntry(Generic[_T]):\n       \"\"\"\n       Provides a ledger entry model.\n       \"\"\"\n       ledger: \"Ledger[_T]\"\n       posting: Posting[_T]\n       balance: Quantity\n\n       @property\n       def date(self) -> datetime.date:\n           \"\"\"\n           Indicates the date of ledger entry.\n           \"\"\"\n           return self.posting.date\n\n       @property\n       def description(self) -> str:\n           \"\"\"\n           Indicates the description of ledger entry.\n           \"\"\"\n           return self.posting.journal.description\n\n       @property\n       def amount(self) -> Amount:\n           \"\"\"\n           Indicates the amount of ledger entry.\n           \"\"\"\n           return self.posting.amount\n\n       @property\n       def cntraccts(self) -> List[Account]:\n           \"\"\"\n           Indicates the counter accounts of ledger entry.\n           \"\"\"\n           return [i.account for i in self.posting.journal.postings if i.account != self.posting.account]\n\n       @property\n       def is_debit(self) -> bool:\n           \"\"\"\n           Indicates if the ledger entry is a debit.\n           \"\"\"\n           return self.posting.is_debit\n\n       @property\n       def is_credit(self) -> bool:\n           \"\"\"\n           Indicates if the ledger entry is a credit.\n           \"\"\"\n           return self.posting.is_credit\n\n       @property\n       def debit(self) -> Optional[Amount]:\n           \"\"\"\n           Indicates the debit amount of ledger entry.\n           \"\"\"\n           return self.amount if self.is_debit else None\n\n       @property\n       def credit(self) -> Optional[Amount]:\n           \"\"\"\n           Indicates the credit amount of ledger entry.\n           \"\"\"\n           return self.amount if self.is_credit else None\n   ```\n\n   Yes, that matches. So `is_debit` just delegates. \n\n   Now write the final test file:\n\n   ```python\n   \"\"\"\n   Tests for `LedgerEntry.is_debit` (pypara/accounting/ledger.py, line 83).\n   \"\"\"\n\n   import datetime\n   from unittest.mock import Mock\n\n   from pypara.accounting.journaling import Direction, JournalEntry, Posting\n   from pypara.accounting.ledger import LedgerEntry\n\n\n   def _make_posting(direction: Direction) -> Posting:\n       \"\"\"\n       Helper that builds a posting with the given direction.\n\n       The account and amount of the posting are irrelevant for the property\n       under test, so simple mocks are used for them.\n       \"\"\"\n       journal = JournalEntry(\n           date=datetime.date(2020, 1, 1),\n           description=\"Test journal entry\",\n           source=None,\n       )\n       return Posting(\n           journal=journal,\n           date=datetime.date(2020, 1, 1),\n           account=Mock(name=\"account\"),\n           direction=direction,\n           amount=Mock(name=\"amount\"),\n       )\n\n\n   def test_ledger_entry_is_debit_for_debit_posting():\n       \"\"\"\n       `LedgerEntry.is_debit` must delegate to the underlying posting when the\n       posting is a debit (i.e. its direction is `INC`).\n       \"\"\"\n       posting = _make_posting(Direction.INC)\n       entry = LedgerEntry(\n           ledger=Mock(name=\"ledger\"),\n           posting=posting,\n           balance=Mock(name=\"balance\"),\n       )\n\n       assert entry.is_debit is True\n       assert entry.is_debit == posting.is_debit\n\n\n   def test_ledger_entry_is_debit_for_credit_posting():\n       \"\"\"\n       `LedgerEntry.is_debit` must delegate to the underlying posting when the\n       posting is a credit (i.e. its direction is `DEC`).\n       \"\"\"\n       posting = _make_posting(Direction.DEC)\n       entry = LedgerEntry(\n           ledger=Mock(name=\"ledger\"),\n           posting=posting,\n           balance=Mock(name=\"balance\"),\n       )\n\n       assert entry.is_debit is False\n       assert entry.is_debit == posting.is_debit\n   ```\n\n   Wait \u2014 `assert entry.is_debit is True`: `posting.is_debit` returns `self.direction == Direction.INC` \u2192 bool True. So `is True` holds. For DEC: `Direction.DEC == Direction.INC` \u2192 False \u2192 `is False` holds. OK.\n\n   But what if `Direction` in this pypara version has different member names? E.g., `Direction.DR`/`CR`? Hmm. In pypara, I'm quite sure it's `INC` and `DEC` because `JournalEntry.increments`/`decrements` properties exist (from get_info) \u2014 those likely filter on `posting.direction == Direction.INC` / `DEC`. The presence of `increments` and `decrements` strongly suggests `INC`/`DEC` members. Good.\n\n   Also, `Direction.of` exists in pypara: `Direction.of(True)` \u2192 INC. But I won't rely on it.\n\n   Alternative safer approach